# Generic configuration options for serial ports
config SERIAL
    bool
config SERIAL_BAUD
    depends on SERIAL
    int "Baud rate for serial port" if LOW_LEVEL_OPTIONS
    default 250000
    help
        Specify the baud rate of the serial port. This should be set
        to 250000. Read the FAQ before changing this value.

# Generic configuration options for the serial mux (MUX) and for bridge mode
# (BRIDGE).
#
# MUX is the device half: a firmware that can be addressed inside a shared byte
# stream - it answers the scan/admin channel, adopts the node id it is handed,
# and handles an id collision the way the CAN code does
# (generic/canserial.c:can_id_conflict). Every firmware wants it, whatever
# carries the stream: MUX_SERIAL for the serial consoles, MUX_CAN for the byte
# stream CAN's nodes reassemble (generic/canserial.c).
#
# MUX, MUX_SERIAL and MUX_CAN are capabilities, not per-arch selections: whoever
# has the wiring selects them, and an arch without the wiring simply leaves them
# off. The wiring lives with whoever carries the byte stream, so each carrier is
# offered wherever that carrier is.
#
# MUX_SERIAL, wherever a console like it exists: the host library's console task
# (src/host/console.c, which HOST_MUX_SERIAL selects), the generic serial
# console the micro-controller arches share (src/generic/serial_irq.c, which
# linux also has a SERIAL for but does not use - it brings its own console in
# src/linux/console.c), that process console itself, and the USB CDC console
# (src/generic/usb_cdc.c). That is the `depends on SERIAL || USBSERIAL` below -
# a transport whose console has no such wiring (ar100, pru have none of their
# own) has no such symbol to depend on and cannot select it, so the choice never
# becomes a link error.
#
# MUX_CAN, wherever CAN is selected (CANSERIAL or USBCANBUS, which is what
# CANBUS means below). Its carrier is the byte stream generic/canserial.c
# reassembles out of the frames on this node's own arbitration id, and it needs
# one for a reason the serial carriers do not have: a bridge reached over CAN
# carries several downstream devices' frames in that one byte stream, so a frame
# with no unit header has nothing to attribute it to and nothing can be routed.
# The single-frame admin channel on CANBUS_ID_ADMIN (0x3f0) is not that channel
# and does not replace it - it stays the byte-level scan/bootloader conversation
# it has always been, and the two do not collide (a header field value inside
# the byte stream is not an arbitration id).
#
# The device also has to have an identity to answer a scan with, so both ask for
# one: MUX_SERIAL as `depends on HAVE_CHIPID || MACH_HOST || MACH_LINUX`, MUX_CAN
# as `depends on HAVE_CHIPID` - it is a micro-controller carrier, and its
# identity is the same six bytes the CAN uuid is derived from
# (generic/chipid_uuid.h). On a micro-controller the arch's chipid.c derives it
# from the chip id (the same HAVE_CHIPID the USB serial number uses); the host
# library takes it from its caller (klipper_host_set_uuid), and the linux
# process from its command line (-U), so MACH_HOST and MACH_LINUX are the other
# ways to have one. An arch with none of these - avr, hc32f460, simulator have
# no chipid.c - would answer with an all-zero identity, which names nothing: the
# option is not offered there rather than offered broken.
#
# It is off by default, on purpose. On a micro-controller it changes the
# console's wire format from bare frames to units, and units are not what the
# upstream klippy host speaks, so a board that turns it on is a board whose host
# has been told to expect the mux - a deliberate choice, never a default. The
# host library is the other side of that call: it is a library and the host
# decides, so HOST_MUX_SERIAL (src/host/Kconfig) turns it on there.
#
# BRIDGE is the router on top of it: it owns several ports - its own klipper
# instance is one of them - and forwards each addressed unit to the port that
# owns the id. A build that already has the mux half of a byte stream has what a
# bridge needs, so BRIDGE comes with it (the same `default y if` shape CANBUS
# uses below). Each carrier selects MUX, so the condition is spelled as the
# terms that imply it - `MUX_SERIAL || MUX_CAN`. Writing `MUX &&` in front of a
# term makes kconfig read that term's own `select MUX` and this default as a
# dependency loop, and either term implies MUX by itself.
#
# Which ports the bridge can be given is the arch's to say. BRIDGE_PORT_SERIAL
# is that: this arch's UART objects (src/uart.c) can be handed to the bridge as
# downstream ports; nothing opens a device on its own. The host names which
# stream such an object carries, and at what rate, at configuration time.
config MUX
    bool
config MUX_SERIAL
    bool "Serial mux: address this console in a shared byte stream"
    depends on SERIAL || USBSERIAL
    depends on HAVE_CHIPID || MACH_HOST || MACH_LINUX
    select MUX
    help
        Make this build's console byte stream speak the serial mux: answer a
        scan with this device's identity, adopt the node id it is handed, head
        its own frames with the reply header, and shut down on an id collision
        (src/mux.c). A build that speaks the mux is also a bridge (BRIDGE's
        `default y if` below).

        Needs an identity to answer with: a micro-controller derives it from
        its chip id (the arch's chipid.c, HAVE_CHIPID), the host library takes
        it from its caller (klipper_host_set_uuid), and the linux process from
        its command line (-U). An arch whose identity would always be zero is
        not offered this option.

        Off by default. With it the console's wire format is units, not the
        bare frames the upstream klippy host speaks, so only a host that
        expects the mux should ever be pointed at this board.
config MUX_CAN
    bool "CAN byte stream mux: address this node in a shared byte stream"
    depends on CANBUS
    depends on HAVE_CHIPID
    select MUX
    help
        Make the byte stream this build reassembles out of CAN speak the
        serial mux: the bytes that arrive on this node's own arbitration id
        are resolved as units (src/generic/canserial.c: mux_input), this node
        answers a scan with its identity, adopts the node id it is handed,
        heads its own frames with the reply header, and shuts down on an id
        collision (src/mux.c). A build that speaks the mux is also a bridge
        (BRIDGE's `default y if` below).

        A CAN upstream has to carry units where a serial one only may: the
        arbitration id names this node, so every frame behind it - one bridge
        relaying several downstream devices - lands in the one byte stream,
        and a frame with no header says nothing about which device sent it.
        The single-frame admin channel (CANBUS_ID_ADMIN, 0x3f0) does not cover
        that: it is addressed to nodes on the bus, not to the devices behind
        this one, and it stays exactly the scan/bootloader conversation it has
        always been.

        Needs an identity to answer with: it is derived from the chip id (the
        arch's chipid.c, HAVE_CHIPID), the same six bytes the canbus uuid is.

        Off by default. With it the wire format is units, not the bare frames
        the upstream klippy host speaks, so only a host that expects the mux
        should ever be pointed at this board.

        MUX_SERIAL and MUX_CAN never hold at once in practice: an arch that
        offers CAN puts its serial and CAN transports in one `choice`, so
        SERIAL and CANBUS exclude each other. That is also what makes
        `src-$(CONFIG_MUX) += mux.c` in src/Makefile list mux.c exactly once;
        an arch with both carriers would otherwise list a source twice and
        duplicate the objects.
config BRIDGE
    bool
    select MUX
    default y if MUX_SERIAL || MUX_CAN
config BRIDGE_PORT_SERIAL
    bool
# The same for CAN: one CAN controller of this build (generic/bridge_can.c) is a
# downstream port, so the devices on that bus are reached through the bridge's
# port commands instead of being nodes of this firmware's own CAN. It is a UART
# object all the same - the host creates it with config_uart and attaches it with
# config_bridge_port - so the object layer comes with it; only the rate is not a
# UART's (CONFIG_CANBUS_FREQUENCY, not the baud the host asks for).
config BRIDGE_PORT_CAN
    bool
    select UART

# The byte-stream UART object layer (src/uart.c): an oid a host creates at
# configuration time, the line rate it asked for, and the seam an arch fills.
config UART
    bool
# A byte stream an arch can attach to a UART object: the host library's tty
# backend (src/host/bridge.c) today. An arch selects this once it can hand a
# UART object out as a bridge port.
config SERIAL_UART
    bool
    select UART
    default y if BRIDGE_PORT_SERIAL

